FIFA Extraplanetary Football Blueprint v1.1

The Dribble Preservation Standard (DPS) — Final System Architecture and Laws

---

Design Objective (Non-Negotiable)

The system exists to preserve Earth-equivalent dribbling dynamics under reduced gravity and near-vacuum aerodynamic conditions. Every hardware and rule choice is evaluated against four invariants:

1. Touch‑timing invariance (ball‑foot rhythm)
2. Lateral cut force equivalence
3. Plant‑foot stability equivalence
4. Predictable ball‑return latency

If any one of these breaks, dribbling disappears. Everything that follows derives from that single constraint.

---

Layer 0: DPS‑0 Matchball (Active Ball System)

The ball is a mechatronic system that regulates restitution and generates artificial aerodynamic lift.

Bladder and Restitution Control

· Core: Multi‑concentric chambers filled with a low‑density electrorheological (ER) fluid. Ultra‑thin carbon‑nanotube electrodes line the inner walls.
· Operation: When a voltage (0–5 kV mm⁻¹) is applied, the fluid’s viscosity rises instantly, sharply increasing internal damping. In the OFF (low‑viscosity) state the ball is lively; in the ON (high‑viscosity) state it is deliberately soft.
· State‑dependent restitution:
  · Dribble mode (ON): effective restitution coefficient e = 0.62 ± 0.02 — matches the derived Mars‑cadence requirement, keeping the ball close and touch rhythm constant.
  · Strike mode (OFF): e = 0.88 ± 0.03 — restores energy transfer for passes and shots, preventing the “dead lead‑weight” effect.
· Transition: Contact‑force threshold 150 N, contact‑duration threshold >12 ms triggers strike mode; shorter contacts default to dribble mode. State change completes in <3 ms.

Magnus Effect Emulator

Mars’ atmosphere (ρ ≈ 0.020 kg m⁻³) produces negligible lift on a spinning ball. The solution is an internal Tri‑Axial Control Moment Gyroscope (CMG).

· Location: A carbon‑fibre gimbal‑suspended flywheel assembly at the geometric centre of the ball.
· Sensing: A 9‑axis IMU (1 kHz) tracks the ball’s shell spin vector ω and free‑flight trajectory.
· Actuation: The CMG precesses to exert a controlled internal torque on the ball body, producing a net lateral force F_sim proportional to ω × v, exactly mimicking the Magnus lift equation. Lateral force authority up to 0.15 N at ball speeds of 30 m s⁻¹.
· Performance: Achieves trajectory error <5% relative to Earth‑equivalent spin‑curves.

Power and Status

· Battery: 3.7 V, 500 mAh solid‑state cell, inductively rechargeable on the pitch mat; 120 min active life.
· On‑ball indicator: Amber LED array and acoustic pulse signal any system fault (e.g., CMG saturation, latency spike >2.5 ms).

