Layers 2 & 3: Vector Boot and Inertial Equilibrium Vest

The player‑wearable hardware is a coordinated lower‑and‑upper‑body system.

D‑Boot (Phase‑Separated Outsole)

· Outsole zones:
  · Touch zone (medial forefoot, toe box): soft rubber for ball feel, no magnetic elements.
  · Plant zone (heel, lateral forefoot): compliant ferromagnetic pads that engage the MRE pitch field when energised.
· Midsole: 16‑cell force‑sensitive resistor matrix (1 kHz) plus 9‑axis IMU.
· Phase classification: Onboard logic detects ground contact from FSR and IMU jerk, classifying the event as “dribble touch” or “plant” within 3 ms (pressure pattern, predicted duration). The state is transmitted wirelessly to pitch and vest systems (UWB, <2 ms latency).
· MRE studs: Each stud contains magnetorheological elastomer whose stiffness is controlled by an induction pickup coil integrated into the boot sole. During touch phase the studs remain compliant; during plant phase they rigidify in lock‑step with the pitch field, driving effective friction toward Earth levels.

IEV (Inertial Equilibrium Vest)

Perfectly anchoring the feet in low gravity leaves the torso vulnerable to catastrophic over‑rotation. The IEV solves this.

· Form: Carbon‑fibre harness with twin high‑speed control moment gyroscopes (CMGs) mounted on the dorsal plate, close to the spine. Total weight 1.8 kg.
· Actuator: Each CMG spins a 400 g titanium flywheel at up to 15,000 RPM. Gimbal torque motors provide peak counter‑torque of 250 Nm (yaw) and 150 Nm (roll).
· Control: The pitch prediction server calculates the required counter‑torque from the player’s biomechanical model and intended cut direction, and sends the torque vector to the vest 10 ms before plant strike. The vest applies the corrective torque within 5 ms, actively balancing the upper body throughout the cut, then decays over 200 ms.
· Safety: Redundant mechanical brakes. If the system fails, flywheels lock instantly; the vest becomes a passive weight. The referee’s deadman switch can deactivate any vest remotely.

